  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery showed stratus dissolving rapidly, and\nscattered cirrus approaching from the west. South winds will\nincrease through this afternoon, gusting 30-40 mph, as leeside\ncyclogenesis gradually organizes over eastern Colorado.\nTemperatures will warm easily well above normal, well into the\n60s, this afternoon. \n\nSouth winds will remain elevated to strong through tonight, as\npressure and height gradients continue to tighten ahead of a\nstrong trough approaching through the Great Basin. Kept south\nwinds near the strongest guidance tonight, near 20 mph sustained\nthrough sunrise Friday, with the boundary layer remaining well\nmixed. With a strong low level jet in place, radiational cooling\nwill be strongly deterred, and temperatures will struggle to\nfall below 50 Friday morning. Record warm minimum temperatures\nin late November are in the 48-52 range, while normal lows are\nin the upper 20s. \n\nEven stronger south winds are expected Friday, as the leeside\ncyclone deepens further over northeast Colorado. Models project\na mslp gradient of near 10 mb across the DDC CWA 3 pm Friday,\nand given a warm, well-mixed boundary layer, 850 mb flow of\n40-45 kts will easily be mixed to the ground in gusts. NBM wind\ngrids were near 12z MAV guidance, so were accepted. \n\nAs one shortwave passes north of SW KS Friday, another stronger\nshortwave carves through the larger longwave trough to near the\nFour Corners 6 am Saturday. Associated surface low pressure will\nbe over SW KS the first half of Saturday with dramatically\nweaker winds. Model consensus suggests cold front will race\nthrough SW KS daytime Saturday with strong north winds. Given\nthe diurnally mixed timing, these north winds will probably be\nstronger than NBM suggests. Temperatures will display a strong\ngradient Saturday, ranging from 40s north to 60s southeast. \n\nIt is difficult for SW KS to receive significant precipitation\nfrom progressive shortwaves in the cool season. Still, with such\nstrong dynamics timed favorably with the diurnal heating cycle,\nscattered to numerous showers are expected Saturday \nafternoon/evening across the southeast 1/2 of SW KS. Little if\nany rain is expected west of US 283. Indeed, a weak triple \npoint may be across/near the southeast zones on the advancing \ncold front Saturday afternoon, supporting a stronger \nthunderstorm or two, per SPC Day 3 marginal 5% severe \nprobability. Both moisture and residency time will be limited, \nand limited to the southeast counties. \n\nAfter an unusually mild to warm autumn, finally much cooler air\nwill prevail Sunday through early next week. Beginning Sunday,\nafternoon temperatures in the 40s and sunrise temperatures in\nthe 20s will prevail. These typical temperatures for late\nNovember will come as quite a shock, and will finally deliver\nseveral widespread hard/killing freezes in the 20s.",
